# tools/cr/crypto_mldsa_sign_witness.rish -- the ML-DSA signing rung: FIPS 204 ML-DSA.Sign_internal, authored in pure Rye and proven byte-for-byte against
# Zig's independent std.crypto sign.mldsa deterministic signer. This is the ML-DSA ladder's crux -- it composes the GREEN base ring
# (crypto/mldsa_ring.rye), serialization and rounding (crypto/mldsa_encode.rye), samplers (crypto/mldsa_sample.rye), and SHAKE (crypto/shake.rye), decodes
# the keygen rung's secret key, and answers a message with a signature a second, independent implementation confirms to the byte (Season G - Cryptography).
#
# Season: crypto -- the ML-DSA ladder's signing rung, the door a Kumara identity signs its name through once the old curves fall. The sign function holds
# no key: every seed and message is a public test vector. Every ML-DSA signature a keeper's name will one day carry is exactly these bytes, so this
# witness proves the whole composition agrees with the NIST-standard reference.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-161537_post-quantum-mlkem-mldsa-pivot.md ("the ML-DSA ladder ... mldsa_sign.rye/mldsa_verify.rye ... proven against
# Zig's std.crypto ML-DSA-44/65/87 and the FIPS-204 KAT. Its front door is the post-quantum successor to Kumara's Ed25519 identity").
# Rye-first spine: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md.
# Clean-room: the ML-DSA.Sign algorithm, the ExpandMask/SampleInBall/MakeHint wiring, and the signature serialization are the public NIST standard's own;
# the arithmetic is our own Rye composed over our own GREEN rungs, with Zig's std.crypto read only as the reference to diff against -- never a copied line
# (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/mldsa_sign.rye proves itself by a DOUBLED oracle -- the discipline the whole library rests on. ML-DSA signing is DETERMINISTIC once rnd is fixed
# (the FIPS deterministic variant sets rnd = 0): the rejection loop walks the same attempts every run, so exactly one signature answers a given (sk, M).
# FIPS 204 specifies standard-domain arithmetic while Zig's std.crypto computes internally in Montgomery domain, yet both normalize to the canonical
# residue before packing, so the two agree byte-for-byte at the published boundary -- the encoded signature. This rung diffs our pure-Rye signature against
# Zig's newKeyFromSeed then sk.signer(null) then finalize then toBytes at all three parameter sets over six deterministic seeds and two messages each.
# Purely local -- no key held, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_mldsa_sign_witness.rish

say "crypto-mldsa-sign: Language EN — the ML-DSA signing rung: FIPS 204 ML-DSA.Sign_internal. It decodes the secret key, computes mu = H(tr then 0x00 0x00 then M) and rho'' = H(K then rnd then mu) with rnd = 0 (the deterministic variant), and runs the rejection loop: draw the mask y = ExpandMask(rho'', kappa), form w = invNTT(A pointwise-multiply NTT(y)), commit to c~ = H(mu then w1Encode(HighBits(w))), draw the sparse challenge c = SampleInBall(c~), answer z = y + c*s1, and emit a signature c~ then BitPack(z) then HintBitPack(h) only when the z, r0, ct0, and hint-weight bounds all hold. This is the ML-DSA ladder's crux, composing the GREEN ring, encode, sample, and shake rungs — the door a Kumara identity signs its name through once the old curves fall."
say "crypto-mldsa-sign: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively as byte parity. ML-DSA signing is deterministic once rnd is fixed, so the rejection loop walks the same attempts every run and exactly one signature answers a given (sk, M). FIPS 204 specifies standard-domain arithmetic; Zig's std.crypto computes in Montgomery domain, yet both normalize to the canonical residue before packing, so the two agree byte-for-byte at the published signature boundary. Our pure-Rye signature must equal Zig's deterministic signer at every parameter set. Two strangers confirming one answer is the discipline the whole library rests on."
say "crypto-mldsa-sign: Lens TAME — bounded loops (k rows, l columns, 256 coefficients, at most 576 rejection attempts), contract asserts on every function, canonical residues in [0, q) throughout, the centered infinity norm measured exactly as ML-DSA's rejection tests do. Correctness proven by a DOUBLED oracle: our standard-domain composition versus Zig's independent Montgomery-domain std.crypto sign.mldsa deterministic signer, byte-for-byte at ML-DSA-44 (k=4 l=4 eta=2 tau=39 omega=80), ML-DSA-65 (k=6 l=5 eta=4 tau=49 omega=55), and ML-DSA-87 (k=8 l=7 eta=2 tau=60 omega=75) over 6 deterministic seeds and 2 messages each, a trivial all-zero signature refused. The sign function calls no std.crypto; only the selftest invokes Zig as the parity oracle. Every seed and message is a public test vector — no key held, no network, no funds, no device; signing with the maintainer's own post-quantum key stays the custody gate, constant-time timing-safety the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/mldsa_sign"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/mldsa_sign.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-mldsa-sign: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/mldsa_sign.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-mldsa-sign: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-mldsa-sign: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-mldsa-sign' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-mldsa-sign: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-mldsa-sign line"
